body:
# Example Section Draft with Handoff Metadata

This worked example shows a complete section_draft output from the section-drafter agent, including the full JSON handoff structure that merge-normalizer consumes. It demonstrates the correct format for content delivery, voice notes, assumptions, and issue flagging.

---

## Full Section Draft JSON

```json
{
  "section_id": "S4",
  "title": "The Three Failure Modes That Kill Recommendation Systems",
  "outline_entry_ref": "G-EXMP-002 / S4",
  "content": "Most recommendation system implementations do not fail because of bad algorithms. They fail because of three operational problems that appear predictably, at predictable stages, and that no algorithm improvement can fix after the fact.\n\nThe first is cold start. When a user has no interaction history, the system has nothing to recommend from. Products typically handle this badly: they show generic popular content, the user disengages, and the system never accumulates enough signal to learn their preferences. Cold start is not a technical problem — it is an onboarding design problem. The fix is explicit preference collection during signup, not a better algorithm.\n\nThe second is popularity bias. Collaborative filtering learns from the majority of users. If most users engage with the ten most popular items, the system learns to recommend those items to everyone, including users who would prefer something else. Over time, the popular items become more popular and the long tail starves. Popularity bias turns a recommendation system into a slightly personalized bestseller list.\n\nThe third is feedback loops. Acting on recommendations changes user behavior, which changes the training data for the next model iteration. A system that recommends political content to engaged users generates more engagement data for political content, which produces stronger political recommendations in the next training cycle. The loop is invisible unless you instrument for it specifically. By the time it is visible, the data distribution is already badly skewed.",
  "word_count": 226,
  "voice_notes": [
    {
      "location": "Paragraph 2, final sentence",
      "note": "Intentionally stated as a sharp aphorism — 'Cold start is not a technical problem, it is an onboarding design problem.' This aligns with the semi-formal, direct style pack. Preserved through merge."
    },
    {
      "location": "Paragraph 3, final sentence",
      "note": "'Slightly personalized bestseller list' is a deliberate figure of speech. It is specific to the argument and not generic AI-smooth language. Recommend preserving in merge normalization."
    },
    {
      "location": "Paragraph 4",
      "note": "Political content used as the concrete example because it is the most widely documented real-world feedback loop case. Verify this is appropriate for the intended audience context before publication."
    }
  ],
  "assumptions": [
    {
      "id": "A1",
      "description": "Assumed the reader is a product manager or technical lead, not an ML practitioner. Failure mode descriptions are at the detection/impact level, not the algorithmic cause level. If the brief audience differs, the technical depth may need adjustment."
    },
    {
      "id": "A2",
      "description": "Assumed the article does not require citations. Feedback loop description is drawn from widely reported cases (YouTube, Facebook) but no specific source is cited. If the publication standard requires grounding, this section needs sourcing."
    }
  ],
  "issues": [
    {
      "id": "B4-001",
      "type": "missing_source_material",
      "severity": "LOW",
      "description": "The claim that popularity bias 'turns a recommendation system into a slightly personalized bestseller list' is a strong editorial claim. It is grounded in generally accepted understanding of collaborative filtering behavior but would benefit from a cited case study if one is available in the research report. No research report was provided for this run.",
      "blocking": false,
      "recommended_action": "If a research report exists, check for a popularity bias case study and add a parenthetical reference. If not, the claim stands on its current grounding."
    }
  ]
}
```

---

## Annotation: Handoff Format Conventions

**voice_notes:** The section-drafter uses voice_notes to flag intentional stylistic choices that merge-normalizer should not automatically alter. Without these flags, a normalizer applying the style pack might smooth away deliberate rhetorical effects. Flag any figure of speech, aphorism, or tonal departure that is intentional.

**assumptions:** List every non-obvious inference the drafter made about audience, scope, or source material. These become the merge-normalizer's checklist for catching misalignments before they reach QA.

**issues:** Low-severity B4 (missing_source_material) flags that do not block merging but should be resolved before the QA phase. The blocking: false field tells merge-normalizer it can proceed. A blocking: true issue stops merge-normalizer and routes to blockage-handler.
